  "1": {
   "type": "answer",
   "content": "No. VCF Operations CPU|Demand is not unmet demand; on a quiet host the gap to CPU|Usage is reserved capacity, not a wait.\n\nMechanism: VCF Operations CPU|Demand follows the capacity model: per running VM the higher of its demand and its CPU reservation, plus the kernel’s System Resource Reservation. vCenter `cpu.demand` is the scheduler sum-of-consumers view: ideal VM demand with no contention, reservations excluded. ESXi CPU Reserved Capacity is the VM reservations only; it excludes the kernel reservation that VCF Operations adds. vCenter Usage matches VCF Operations Usage in magnitude; the two Demand values differ because vCenter Demand omits VM and kernel reservations while VCF Operations includes them.\n\nReason: the host is quiet with few VMs, so real work is low and Usage ~300-480 MHz in VCF Operations and ~342 MHz average in vCenter. VCF Operations Demand ~2.9 GHz is the reserved capacity floor, not a shortfall. A reservation acts only under contention; unused reserved CPU is used by other VMs. Demand above Usage can be reservation alone. Performance is decided by VM CPU Ready/Co-stop, not by the Demand-Usage gap.\n\nCheck next: VM CPU Ready and Co-stop peaks on the host’s VMs; ESXi CPU Reserved Capacity and the host System Resource Reservation to quantify the reserved floor; ESXi Thread/Core Utilization for free capacity. `vsphere/technology/cpu/metrics/host/demand`; `vsphere/technology/cpu/metrics/host/reserved-capacity`; `vsphere/architecture/provider-consumer`.",
